1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,818
The refund agent from earlier in this section worked perfectly in every demo anyone

2
00:00:04,818 --> 00:00:05,411
showed you.

3
00:00:05,411 --> 00:00:09,771
It also had never hit a network timeout calling the payment system, never seen an

4
00:00:09,771 --> 00:00:14,294
order history come back empty, and never been asked to do the same task twice by two

5
00:00:14,294 --> 00:00:16,070
different support agents at once.

6
00:00:16,070 --> 00:00:18,439
Production hits all three in the first week.

7
00:00:18,439 --> 00:00:20,700
None of those three are exotic edge cases.

8
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:21,562
They're Tuesday.

9
00:00:21,562 --> 00:00:26,030
A network blips, a record is mid-update when something reads it, two people on your

10
00:00:26,030 --> 00:00:27,376
team work the same queue.

11
00:00:27,376 --> 00:00:31,898
Any system that touches real infrastructure meets all three eventually, and the only

12
00:00:31,898 --> 00:00:36,367
real choice you have is whether it meets them with a plan already in place or finds

13
00:00:36,367 --> 00:00:36,851
out live.

14
00:00:36,851 --> 00:00:38,574
None of that is a model problem.

15
00:00:38,574 --> 00:00:42,666
It's an architecture problem, and there are three patterns that consistently

16
00:00:42,666 --> 00:00:47,080
separate the agents that survive contact with production from the ones that don't.

17
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:50,903
Notice none of the three require a better model, and that's deliberate.

18
00:00:50,903 --> 00:00:55,048
Every one of them is a design decision you can ask about and evaluate without

19
00:00:55,048 --> 00:00:59,355
reading a line of code, the same way you'd ask about error handling in any other

20
00:00:59,355 --> 00:01:00,647
feature before it ships.

21
00:01:01,197 --> 00:01:05,943
The difference sounds small and changes everything about how debuggable the system

22
00:01:05,943 --> 00:01:06,117
is.

23
00:01:06,117 --> 00:01:10,746
An agent that decides one step at a time is genuinely reactive: observe, decide,

24
00:01:10,746 --> 00:01:15,434
act, exactly like 6.1's loop, with no plan you could inspect before it commits to

25
00:01:15,434 --> 00:01:15,608
it.

26
00:01:15,608 --> 00:01:20,295
An agent that plans first states, in plain language, what it intends to do and in

27
00:01:20,295 --> 00:01:22,668
what order, before calling a single tool.

28
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:25,504
That plan is cheap to review and cheap to reject.

29
00:01:25,504 --> 00:01:30,018
The refund agent that plans "check order history, confirm no prior refund this

30
00:01:30,018 --> 00:01:34,648
month, then issue the refund" lets you catch a missing check before it runs, not

31
00:01:34,648 --> 00:01:36,442
after it's already moved money.

32
00:01:36,442 --> 00:01:40,840
Does that mean every agent should plan-then-execute, regardless of the task?

33
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:45,644
No. A single lookup barely benefits from a written plan; there's nothing to review.

34
00:01:45,644 --> 00:01:50,331
The pattern earns its cost specifically once a task spans several steps with real

35
00:01:50,331 --> 00:01:55,193
consequences, which describes most of the agents actually worth scoping carefully in

36
00:01:55,193 --> 00:01:56,119
the first place.

37
00:01:56,669 --> 00:02:01,052
Retrying a failed step sounds like the safe, resilient choice, and unbounded

38
00:02:01,052 --> 00:02:05,839
retrying is one of the most common ways an agent quietly turns a small failure into

39
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:06,820
an expensive one.

40
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:09,300
A payment system timing out once is normal.

41
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:13,972
An agent that retries a timed-out refund call five times without checking whether

42
00:02:13,972 --> 00:02:18,413
the first attempt actually went through can issue the same refund five times.

43
00:02:18,413 --> 00:02:23,085
Bound the retries, and more importantly, decide in advance what happens when they

44
00:02:23,085 --> 00:02:23,547
run out.

45
00:02:23,547 --> 00:02:28,392
The honest answer is almost never "keep trying forever." It's "stop, and put this in

46
00:02:28,392 --> 00:02:32,948
front of a person," the same escalation instinct as Section three's degradation

47
00:02:32,948 --> 00:02:37,216
paths, now applied to a system that acts instead of one that only answers.

48
00:02:37,216 --> 00:02:41,196
Notice the diagram has exactly one exit that isn't success: escalate.

49
00:02:41,196 --> 00:02:45,868
Not "try a different approach automatically," which just substitutes one untested

50
00:02:45,868 --> 00:02:50,482
path for another, and not "silently give up," which is 5.5's silent-failure mode

51
00:02:50,482 --> 00:02:52,674
wearing a different section's clothes.

52
00:02:52,674 --> 00:02:57,231
A human seeing the failure and the full context around it is the only exit that

53
00:02:57,231 --> 00:03:01,787
reliably turns a stuck agent into a solved problem instead of a mysterious one.

54
00:03:02,337 --> 00:03:06,970
Say the honest caveat, because plan-then-execute and bounded retries aren't free,

55
00:03:06,970 --> 00:03:11,603
and treating them as a checklist every agent must satisfy regardless of stakes is

56
00:03:11,603 --> 00:03:12,861
its own kind of waste.

57
00:03:12,861 --> 00:03:17,609
An internal agent that drafts a weekly summary nobody reads unattended can probably

58
00:03:17,609 --> 00:03:18,638
skip most of this.

59
00:03:18,638 --> 00:03:23,042
An agent that touches customer refunds, sends external email, or edits a live

60
00:03:23,042 --> 00:03:27,675
document cannot, and the difference between those two is exactly the blast radius

61
00:03:27,675 --> 00:03:32,479
question the guardrails lecture two lectures from now makes the actual scoping tool.

62
00:03:32,479 --> 00:03:37,284
For today, just hold the principle: match the engineering investment to what happens

63
00:03:37,284 --> 00:03:39,514
when this specific agent gets it wrong.

64
00:03:39,514 --> 00:03:43,918
Watch for the opposite mistake too, the one that's easier to make in a budget

65
00:03:43,918 --> 00:03:45,520
conversation than it sounds.

66
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:49,981
A team under time pressure skips the plan-review step or the idempotency check

67
00:03:49,981 --> 00:03:54,786
specifically on the agent that touches money, because that's the one everyone's most

68
00:03:54,786 --> 00:03:55,586
eager to ship.

69
00:03:55,586 --> 00:04:00,162
That's precisely backwards, and it's worth saying out loud in the room where the

70
00:04:00,162 --> 00:04:03,765
schedule gets decided, not after the duplicate refund shows up.

71
00:04:04,315 --> 00:04:08,778
That's the third pattern, and it's the one people skip because it sounds like a

72
00:04:08,778 --> 00:04:11,377
backend detail rather than a product decision.

73
00:04:11,377 --> 00:04:11,886
It isn't.

74
00:04:11,886 --> 00:04:16,349
Whether an action is safe to repeat is exactly as much your call as whether the

75
00:04:16,349 --> 00:04:18,269
agent needs a plan reviewed first.

76
00:04:18,269 --> 00:04:20,473
Run the exercise on something concrete.

77
00:04:20,473 --> 00:04:23,523
"Send a confirmation email" running twice is annoying.

78
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:28,156
"Issue a refund" running twice is a real financial loss, and both can be caused by

79
00:04:28,156 --> 00:04:32,732
the exact same retry that looked like the safe, cautious choice from pattern two.

80
00:04:32,732 --> 00:04:35,613
The fix is usually cheaper than the finding sounds.

81
00:04:35,613 --> 00:04:40,246
Often it's a single check added before the risky call: has this order already been

82
00:04:40,246 --> 00:04:42,958
refunded today, has this email already gone out.

83
00:04:42,958 --> 00:04:47,421
That one addition is what makes pattern two's retries genuinely safe instead of

84
00:04:47,421 --> 00:04:51,884
merely convenient, and it's worth costing out at the same time you cost out the

85
00:04:51,884 --> 00:04:55,951
retry logic itself, not as an afterthought once someone notices the gap.

86
00:04:56,501 --> 00:04:58,138
One thing before you move on.

87
00:04:58,138 --> 00:05:00,452
Ask to see the plan, not just the output.

88
00:05:00,452 --> 00:05:04,855
If there isn't one, because the system decides one step at a time with nothing

89
00:05:04,855 --> 00:05:09,426
written down first, that's worth knowing before you're debugging it in production

90
00:05:09,426 --> 00:05:11,684
instead of reviewing it in a design doc.

91
00:05:11,684 --> 00:05:16,199
Then ask the retry question directly, and expect a specific answer, not a shrug.

92
00:05:16,199 --> 00:05:19,360
"It handles errors gracefully" is not a specific answer.

93
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:22,972
"Three retries, then it stops and pings the on-call channel" is.

94
00:05:22,972 --> 00:05:27,601
Next lecture takes this same production-survival lens and points it at a different

95
00:05:27,601 --> 00:05:32,172
question: when do you actually need more than one agent working together, and why

96
00:05:32,172 --> 00:05:34,938
local-first is usually the safer way to find out.
